or-thin race, pulling the expected margin down from the mid-single-digit consensus. Markets still price Gluesenkamp Perez heavily favored, so a Lean D outlook with a small margin best balances the evidence.", "watch": "Any post-debate or late-October polling; if the new dead-heat surveys are confirmed rather than outliers, this shifts to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 84 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Marie Gluesenkamp Perez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Southwest Washington's swing seat features first-term incumbent Marie Gluesenkamp Perez against state Senator John Braun in a district that leans Republican by about two points. Perez has dominated fundraising, out-raising Braun roughly three to one and holding a cash advantage, while outside spending has been minimal so far. Expert raters split between Tossup and Likely D, and prediction markets price her win probability near 90 percent.\n\nThe picture is complicated by fresh polling: an OPB survey in early October found the two neck-and-neck, and local outlets describe a razor-thin race, even though older surveys had shown Perez ahead. Braun has also sought distance from Trump on tariffs. The forecast lands on a narrow Democratic edge with elevated uncertainty, given the conflicting signals between markets, raters, and the newest polls.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8833, "margin": 7.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8392, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8323, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8357, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8044, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8079, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8641, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8353, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8253, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.79, "margin": 5.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.7681, "margin":